# 21. Real-World Data Engineering HDFS Directory Structure

A data engineer commonly organizes data into different zones.

Example:

```text
/company/
│
├── raw/
│   ├── employees/
│   ├── customers/
│   └── transactions/
│
├── processed/
│   ├── employees/
│   ├── customers/
│   └── transactions/
│
├── curated/
│   ├── reports/
│   └── analytics/
│
├── archive/
│   ├── 2025/
│   └── 2026/
│
└── backup/
```

### Meaning

| Directory | Purpose |
|---|---|
| raw | Original source data |
| processed | Cleaned and transformed data |
| curated | Business-ready datasets |
| archive | Historical data |
| backup | Backup copies |

### Create the structure

```bash
hdfs dfs -mkdir -p /company/raw/employees
hdfs dfs -mkdir -p /company/raw/customers
hdfs dfs -mkdir -p /company/raw/transactions

hdfs dfs -mkdir -p /company/processed
hdfs dfs -mkdir -p /company/curated
hdfs dfs -mkdir -p /company/archive
hdfs dfs -mkdir -p /company/backup
```

Verify:

```bash
hdfs dfs -ls -R /company
```

